import type { TimeBlockContent } from '../simulation'

// Management Consulting - Project Fresca (8-week profit study, week 3).
// Real research-backed V2 content for all three experiential tiers, integrated
// verbatim from the _content-integration/ drafts:
//   - Orientation (3 blocks): consulting-orientation-3block-V2.md
//   - Day in the Life (7 blocks, one Wednesday): consulting-day-in-the-life-V2.md
//   - Full Simulation (30 blocks, Sunday-Friday): consulting-full-simulation-V2.md
// Cast: Priya (EM), Dan (Partner), Marcus (2nd-yr Associate), Jake (PE sponsor VP).
// Prose is copied verbatim from the source files; do not paraphrase or trim.

export const managementConsultantContent: Record<string, TimeBlockContent> = {
  "management-consultant-orientation-b1": {
    before: "Start here, because almost everything people think they know about consulting is either the glamorous version or the cynical version, and the truth is more interesting than both. Before you feel a single day of it in the simulations, get the shape of the thing.",
    simulatedWork: "Companies hire consulting firms when they face a problem too important to get wrong and too hard, too political, or too urgent to solve with the people they have. Profits are shrinking and nobody agrees why. Two companies are merging and someone must make one plus one equal more than two. A private equity firm just bought a business and wants to know, quickly and precisely, what it bought and where the improvement lives.\n\nThe firm sends a small team, typically a partner, an engagement manager, and two to four junior consultants, for a defined engagement of roughly 6 to 12 weeks. The team digs into data, interviews people from store floors to boardrooms, forms an answer, and presents recommendations to senior executives. Then it dissolves and re-forms around the next client, often in a completely different industry. That is the secret most outsiders miss: consulting is a profession of professional learners. You become a temporary expert in restaurant labor costs for eight weeks, then airline pricing, then hospital operations. The product is not industry knowledge. The product is structured problem-solving, applied fast, by people trained and drilled to do it.\n\nStrip away the mystique and a junior consultant's time goes to five things. Analysis: living in Excel and modern data tools, turning a million messy rows into \"40 stores drive 60% of the problem.\" Slides: consulting ships its product as presentations, and building them well is closer to writing than to formatting, one message per page, headlines that sell answers. Meetings and interviews: talking to the client's people to learn what the data cannot say, because much of the real insight comes from a defensive manager finally telling the truth in minute forty. Working with AI tools: every major firm has deployed internal assistants that search decades of firm knowledge in seconds, draft and grade slides, and synthesize research, and most juniors now use them weekly or daily. And getting coached: daily, sometimes hourly feedback from the layer above you, almost always about the work, almost never about you.",
    commentary: "Hold onto two things from this block. First, what clients actually buy is structured problem-solving applied fast, not a binder of industry facts, which is why firms throw you into a new industry every couple of months and expect you to get smart quickly. Second, the AI shift is real and it cuts both ways: the tedious parts of the junior job are shrinking, and so is the army of juniors historically hired to do them. The firms themselves say the junior role is moving toward judgment, field work, and client skills, the things the tools cannot do. Plan your skills accordingly.",
    after: "That is the work. Next, the part recruiting brochures undersell in both directions: the life that surrounds the work, and the machinery that decides how your career inside a firm actually goes.",
  },
  "management-consultant-orientation-b2": {
    before: "Two things shape a consulting career as much as the work itself: the physical rhythm of the job, and two pieces of hidden machinery that almost no candidate has explained to them before they sign. Here they are, plainly.",
    simulatedWork: "The classic rhythm: fly to the client Monday morning, often on a 6 AM flight, work from a client conference room through Thursday, fly home Thursday night, work Friday from your home office. Four days a week in hotels, with the same three or four teammates, for weeks at a stretch. Honest update: travel varies more than it used to. Since the pandemic, some engagements run three days on site, some are mostly remote with periodic visits, and some, notably private-equity and operations work, still run the full classic rhythm. Hours are real, 55 to 65 in a typical week, more before a major client presentation. The travel and the hours end more consulting careers than anything else. What the brochures undersell in the other direction is the closeness: these teams spend more waking hours together than most families, and the camaraderie is consistently what alumni say they miss most.\n\nYou work inside a small, steep pyramid. Analysts and associates (you, years one to three) do the analysis, build the pages, and find the truth in the field. Engagement managers (roughly years three to six) run the team, own the storyline, and absorb the client's pressure, widely considered the hardest job in the building. Partners own the client relationships, sell the work, and are judged on whether clients come back.\n\nNow the two pieces of machinery. First, staffing is a marketplace: projects bid for people, and between engagements you sit \"on the beach,\" restful for a few days but reputationally dangerous if it stretches, because an unbilled consultant is, in the firm's cold arithmetic, inventory. Getting staffed well is a learnable skill built on reputation. Second, reviews run on a cycle with teeth: at top firms you are formally reviewed roughly every six months, feedback gathered from everyone you worked with, a committee of partners sorting people into performance brackets. Persistent bottom-bracket ratings lead to being \"counseled out,\" the velvet term for a managed exit with runway and references. This is the famous up-or-out model, and in a typical entering class a meaningful fraction does not reach the two-year promotion.",
    commentary: "Veterans will tell you the review inputs are not purely meritocratic. Staffing luck, manager quality, and internal sponsorship all matter, which is exactly why they advise treating visibility and relationships as part of the job rather than politics layered on top of it. And notice the travel point: a \"low travel\" firm can still hand you a high-travel year, and the quality of your specific team's norms will shape your life more than anything in the offer letter. These are the two levers that most determine whether a given consulting seat is livable for you.",
    after: "So that is what it is and how it runs. The last block is the honest ledger: what it costs, what it pays, who thrives, who burns out, and how to read your own reactions in the simulations ahead.",
  },
  "management-consultant-orientation-b3": {
    before: "Consulting offers one of the most explicit trades in professional life. Here is the ledger with nothing hidden on either side, and then the only question that actually matters.",
    simulatedWork: "What it costs: the hours and travel above, Sunday evenings that are never fully yours, a performance system that never sleeps, and a feedback culture that thin-skinned people experience as sandpaper. Said plainly: burnout in this industry is real, well documented, and not a moral failing. The old line that everyone is only ever two bad projects away from quitting survives because it contains truth. The sustainable careers belong to people who guard sleep, movement, and a life outside the job.\n\nWhat it pays, at the top strategy firms as of 2026: new-graduate analysts earn roughly $110,000 to $115,000 base with total first-year compensation around $135,000; post-MBA associates earn roughly $190,000 base with first-year totals around $260,000 to $285,000; engagement managers clear $250,000; partners earn from the high six figures into the millions. Figures shift, and top-firm bases have been largely flat for a few years. But the deeper payment is acceleration: presenting to executives a decade before peers in other careers, three industries in eighteen months, and a permanently installed toolkit of structured problem-solving and executive communication. Typical tenure is two to four years, and the industry is consciously built around it. Alumni populate private equity, corporate strategy, startups, and senior corporate leadership, and the firms cultivate those networks deliberately.\n\nWho thrives usually share a few wirings: energized rather than threatened by being the least informed person in the room, which happens constantly; they enjoy making messy things clear; they can hear \"this page is wrong\" without hearing \"you are inadequate\"; and they build allies quickly. Who burns out usually hits one of three walls: the travel corrodes something they value too much, the review intensity reads as constant judgment rather than investment, or they discover they need to own outcomes rather than advise on them. None of these are weaknesses. They are fit signals.",
    commentary: "Here is how to use what comes next. The Day-in-the-Life drops you into one real deck day; the Full Simulation gives you an entire week on a live engagement. As you go through them, watch your own reactions like the data they are. Which moments pull you in? Which exhaust you just reading them? That reaction, projected across two to four years, is the real answer to whether this career fits you, and it is precisely the answer no brochure, ranking, salary table, or thirty-minute coffee chat can give you. Collecting it cheaply, now, is the entire point of this platform.",
    after: "You now hold the map. The honest frame is not \"is this my forever career?\" but \"do I want what two to four years of this buys, at the price it charges?\" Step into the Day-in-the-Life and start gathering your own answer.",
  },
  "management-consultant-dil-d1-b1": {
    before: "You wake in the same mid-tier business hotel as every week, the one where the front desk now greets your team by name, your home four nights a week for this engagement. Today has one shape: build the presentation all morning, survive the partner review at 2, fix whatever it breaks tonight. Tomorrow the deck meets a CEO.",
    simulatedWork: "You move through the travel-week morning on autopilot now, three weeks in. Twenty-five minutes in the small hotel gym, not because you love it but because the second-years preach morning workouts on deck days and they are right: the evening will not be offering one. Shower, lobby coffee, both phones into your pockets, the personal one and the work one, a two-phone existence nobody warned you about. Then the eight-minute walk to Fresca headquarters.\n\nYou run the day in your head as you walk. Your section of tomorrow's deck is six draft pages, and by tonight they have to be bulletproof, because the audience is a CEO, a CFO, and two people from the private equity firm that just spent several hundred million dollars on this company and would like to know, in some detail, what they bought. You can feel the size of that in your chest a little. You decide that is normal.\n\nMarcus falls into step a block out, coffee in hand.\n\nMarcus (Associate): Deck day. Hydrate, guard your snacks, trust no printer.\n\n[It is, you are learning, the closest thing he offers to a good-morning.]",
    commentary: "Consulting weeks have a rhythm, and Wednesday is its summit. Monday you analyze, Tuesday you investigate in the field, Wednesday everything becomes \"the deck\": the slide presentation that is this industry's primary product and the team's entire case to the client. Notice the morning gym call, it is not wellness content, it is load management. Deck days routinely run past twelve hours, and the people who last in this career learn early that energy is rationed like the scarce resource it is. The two phones and the hotel-as-home texture are the lifestyle's fixed costs; whether they feel like adventure or erosion varies by person, and noticing your own reaction is part of why you are here.",
    after: "Through the lobby, up to the windowless conference room the client surrendered to your team, every engagement has one, it is always called the war room, where Priya is already at the whiteboard writing the day's plan.",
  },
  "management-consultant-dil-d1-b2": {
    before: "Before anyone builds a single slide, the team agrees on the story. Priya runs this from the whiteboard, where a numbered outline with dashes under each number is waiting. Consultants call it a dot-dash: every \"dot\" a page headline, every \"dash\" the evidence beneath. The deck gets written this way, as headlines, while changing it still costs nothing.",
    simulatedWork: "[Scene: the war room. Priya at the whiteboard, a numbered outline already up with dashes under each line. You and Marcus at the table, laptop lids low.]\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): Twenty-five pages, three chapters. Chapter one, where the margin went. Chapter two, the two engines, pricing and labor. Chapter three, the path to the sponsor's thirty million. Jake's Managing Director is flying in for this, so assume the toughest version of every question.\n\n[She runs the outline fast, marker tapping each dot. At your labor section she compresses six pages to five and reorders them, evidence first, then mechanism. You feel the disagreement before you have decided to say it.]\n\nAnalyst (Consulting): I'd lead with the mechanism, not the evidence. The forecast gap is what makes the forty stores make sense. It's also the thing the COO's team gets attacked over if we sequence it wrong.\n\n[Priya stops. Studies the board for three full seconds. Marcus does not look up, but his pen stops moving.]\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): You're right. And the political point is the better reason, not the logic. Mechanism first.\n\n[She erases two dots and rewrites them in the new order. It is a small thing. It does not feel small. Across the table Marcus raises one eyebrow a quarter inch, which from him is a parade.]\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): Your five pages to me by 12:30 so I can assemble before Dan at 2. Marcus, pricing on the same clock.\n\n[She caps the marker, then adds the day's other rule like an afterthought that isn't one.]\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): Protected lunch. Twenty real minutes, away from the laptop. Long day. Start it like an adult.",
    commentary: "The dot-dash is one of consulting's core rituals: debate the argument while it is words on a whiteboard, because changing a headline costs nothing and changing a finished slide costs an afternoon. Notice the pushback that won: a three-month associate disagreed with the manager, attached one reason rooted in the client's politics, and the storyline changed. Good teams genuinely run this way, hierarchy decides, ideas compete as equals, and the fastest way junior people earn standing is exactly this: disagreeing rarely, briefly, and with a reason attached. The protected lunch is worth stealing for any career; teams that survive deck days without fraying have managers who ration collective energy on purpose.",
    after: "Headphones on, calendar blocked. Four and a half hours to turn five headlines into five pages that can survive a partner.",
  },
  "management-consultant-dil-d1-b3": {
    before: "The longest unbroken stretch of craft in the week: rough pages become finished ones. Real exhibits, traced numbers, headlines that earn their rent. The turnover analysis you pulled last night, crew quits in your 40 stores running roughly 20 points above the chain's already-brutal fast-casual baseline, gets built in as your section's second engine.",
    simulatedWork: "This is the longest unbroken stretch of craft in the week, and a quiet part of you loves it: rough pages becoming finished ones, real exhibits, traced numbers, headlines that earn their rent. You build in priority order, and you can feel your own focus narrow to the width of the screen.\n\nThe cliff chart first, rebuilt clean: 240 stores ranked by normalized overtime, your 40 shaded, one callout. You write the headline as a full sentence, the way Priya drilled into you, \"Overtime is not a chain-wide problem: 40 stores drive over 60% of the increase,\" because a headline that only labels the chart is a wasted line.\n\nThen the page you are quietly proudest of: the forecast-gap diagram, the system seeing register sales on the left, blind to the ribbon of mobile orders on the right, and the human workarounds that follow. You hold it to one bar: the COO's people should grasp it in ten seconds and feel described, not accused. You strip every word of jargon off it twice.\n\nThe sizing build folds in turnover, total prize roughly $11M. Then the modern reflex: you run the pages through the firm's internal AI slide-reviewer, which grades them against best-practice templates. It catches two things, an evidence page with two messages fighting for one headline (a genuinely good catch, you split it) and a footnote font that has drifted. The machine is your most pedantic coworker, and it is never wrong, which is somehow worse.\n\nAt 11:50, Marcus on Slack: \"Trade you a sanity check. My elasticity page for your turnover math.\" You swap, each find one soft spot, each fix it. Five pages to Priya at 12:20. Her reply: \"Solid. Assembling. Dan at 2. Be in the room, both of you.\"",
    commentary: "This is the craft block, and people who like making things tend to secretly love it: hours of focus where a week of mess becomes pages with one message each, stated as a full-sentence headline, provable from the exhibit beneath. If a page needs a person standing next to it to make sense, it fails. Two textures of the modern job are on display. The AI slide-reviewer is exactly the kind of tool now embedded at every major firm, used weekly by most junior consultants for research synthesis, drafting, and quality review, and the associate's mixed feelings, gratitude plus a flicker of \"what does this mean for people like me\", is the live conversation at every junior level in the industry right now. The defensible ground is the judgment the tool cannot supply: which decomposition, which evidence, which one sentence. The peer swap is the older technology: the person who built a page is the worst person alive to proofread it, so good teams trade.",
    after: "Protected lunch, as ordered: twenty minutes in the client's test kitchen arguing about the new menu item. At 2 PM, Dan reviews the deck. You have been told partner reviews are an experience. Time to find out.",
  },
  "management-consultant-dil-d1-b4": {
    before: "Partner review. Dan has 90 minutes between client meetings to judge 25 pages that took the team three days to build. Priya projects; you and Marcus sit with notebooks. You are not presenting. It turns out that does not mean you are safe.",
    simulatedWork: "[Scene: the war room. Priya drives the deck from the laptop. You and Marcus sit with notebooks. Dan stays standing, jacket on, phone face-down, and flips through all 25 pages in roughly two minutes, silent, reading only headlines, before back to page one.]\n\nDan (Partner): The story is buried. Page one has to do all the work: a credible path to the thirty million, two engines, what happens next. The CEO should be able to walk out after one page and repeat our answer to his board word for word. Right now page one is an agenda, and agendas are for meetings nobody remembers.\n\n[Then your section.]\n\nDan (Partner): Cliff chart, keep it, best evidence in the deck. Mechanism diagram, move it earlier, it's the best page in here.\n\n[A half-second of relief. Then, no change in tone:]\n\nDan (Partner): Kill these three. The field-evidence quotes, the methodology page, the sizing build. Quotes to appendix, this sponsor distrusts anecdotes on principle. Methodology to appendix, nobody cares how the sausage was made until they doubt the sausage. The sizing becomes one number on the summary page. In a forty-minute meeting every page is rent, and three of yours aren't paying.\n\n[Nine seconds, three pages, most of your morning. It stings somewhere physical, under the sternum. Across the table, Priya catches your eye: normal, write it down, keep moving. You write it down. Dan stands, gathering his jacket.]\n\nDan (Partner): Good deck underneath. Answer-first by tonight.\n\n[At the door, to Priya but looking at you:]\n\nDan (Partner): The overtime number. Who owns it?\n\n[Priya nods your way. One second of held eye contact.]\n\nDan (Partner): Know it cold tomorrow. The MD will go there.",
    commentary: "Every consultant alive remembers their first partner review, usually with a wince, eventually with gratitude, so decode what Dan actually did. He was not grading effort; he was editing for four people and forty minutes of attention, and against that constraint, pages that answer unasked questions are not neutral, they are rent-takers. The killed pages were good work, which is exactly why they move to the appendix, where they silently back every number if anyone doubts the sausage, rather than the recycling bin. Learning to cut work you are proud of without bleeding ego on the floor is one of this job's hardest and most transferable lessons; the people who defend pages like territory plateau early regardless of intelligence. And \"know it cold\" from a partner is not advice. It means tomorrow, in some form, that number will be yours, live, in front of the client.",
    after: "The rebuild list is long and the deadline is tonight. Coffee run, sleeves up, and a strange thing happens to the room's mood: it improves. There is a specific energy that arrives when a team knows exactly what to fix.",
  },
  "management-consultant-dil-d1-b5": {
    before: "The deck restructures around Dan's edits: a new executive summary carrying the entire story, your section reordered, three pages demoted to appendix, every headline rewritten to lead with the answer. Priya takes the summary. You take your section. Marcus takes pricing.",
    simulatedWork: "The reordering is mechanical. The headline rewriting is not, and you can feel yourself learning in real time, the way you feel a muscle the first day you use it.\n\nOld headline: \"Analysis of overtime drivers across the store network.\" New: \"Fixing the labor forecast unlocks ~$11M with no service impact.\" The first describes your work. The second sells an answer. Once you see the difference you cannot unsee it, and you go hunting back through every page for description disguised as argument. You find it twice more, and each time it is a small embarrassment that you wrote it the slow way first.\n\nAt 6:30, Priya posts her summary page to the channel: the entire eight-week study on one page, three numbers, three sentences, the kind of simple that is obviously expensive. You say so on the channel.\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): Eighth draft. The first seven were garbage. That is the entire job.\n\nAround 7, a ripple from outside: Jake emails Dan asking whether the team can \"walk the sponsor through preliminary findings tonight, just informally.\" You watch the politics resolve on the channel in real time, Dan declining warmly in two sentences, the team will see them at SteerCo with finished work, copying no one. Marcus, deadpan, to you only: \"And that, children, is why partners get paid.\" By 7:40 your section is rebuilt, your slain pages settled into the appendix, demoted but load-bearing.",
    commentary: "\"Answer-first\" is the deepest habit this industry installs, and it follows people for life, into emails, interviews, eventually into how they think: most professions present evidence and then conclude; consulting concludes and then proves, because executives decide in thirty seconds whether the next thirty are worth having. Hold onto \"eighth draft\": the polish in final decks is not talent, it is iteration nobody puts on a recruiting poster. And study the side-door maneuver, because no training module teaches it: the sponsor tried to pull unfinished work out early, and the partner declined in under forty words while protecting the team, preserving the relationship, and conceding nothing. Knowing what to say is a skill. Knowing what not to send before Thursday is a career.",
    after: "The deck is structurally sound. What remains is the final mile, numbers traced, pages aligned, the whole story read aloud, and the team orders room service to the war room. The energy, against all logic, is good.",
  },
  "management-consultant-dil-d1-b6": {
    before: "Big presentations do not end at \"done,\" they end at \"checked.\" Tonight: every number traced to source, every page footnoted, the deck read start to finish aloud. The filename is on its way from v7 to v9, and someone has added \"_FINAL\" to a draft as a joke, because in this industry \"FINAL\" in a filename is the most reliable lie there is.",
    simulatedWork: "[Scene: the war room, late. Room-service plates pushed to one end of the table. The deck is on the screen, filename creeping from v7 toward v9, and someone has added \"_FINAL\" to an earlier draft as a joke. Priya reads every headline aloud in sequence, no slides, just the words, testing whether the story stands with the pictures stripped away.]\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): \"Margin fell four points in eighteen months.\" Good. \"Two engines drove it, pricing and labor.\" Good. \"Fixing the labor forecast unlocks eleven million with no service impact.\"\n\n[She stops. Frowns at her own sentence.]\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): That one snags. \"Unlocks\" is soft. Rewrite it tonight.\n\n[You and Marcus swap sections and trace every number to source, doctrine by now: the person who built a page is the worst person alive to proofread it.]\n\nMarcus (Associate): (at 9:10, quiet) Hey. Your sizing footnote says nine point two. The model says nine point seven.\n\nAnalyst (Consulting): That's a version-four fossil.\n\nMarcus (Associate): Yep.\n\n[You trace it, fix it, initial the checklist, and pay him in the last of the room-service fries.]\n\nMarcus (Associate): That one would've been tomorrow's entire meeting.\n\n[He's right, and the fries are not enough, and you both know it. At 9:45 the slap-happy hour arrives on schedule. Marcus starts narrating the page transitions in a movie-trailer voice.]\n\nMarcus (Associate): In a world... where forecasts cannot see burritos...\n\n[For two full minutes nobody in the room can breathe. It is, genuinely, one of the best moments of the week, and a quiet part of you files it under reasons people stay in this job longer than the hours justify. At 10:30, version 9 is done. Priya looks at it a long moment.]\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): Good. Both of you, bed. I'll do the final read and send to Dan tonight. Dry run at 7:30.\n\nAnalyst (Consulting): I can stay and help with the rebuild list.\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): No. (the tone closes the topic) Tomorrow needs you sharp, not loyal. Go.\n\n[Walking out, you glance back: she's settling in with the deck and a fresh coffee. You understand something new about what the manager job actually costs.]",
    commentary: "Here is the honest picture of consulting hours, no recruiting gloss: this is a 14-plus-hour day, and presentation weeks contain one or two of them. Typical weeks run 55 to 65 hours, crunch weeks more; anyone who says otherwise wants something from you. Inside the grind, two things deserve attention. The checking culture, numbers traced, initialed, owned, exists because one wrong number in front of a sponsor poisons every right one; precision here is not perfectionism, it is the product itself. And the end of the night shows you one of the industry's unwritten compacts: seniors spending their seniority as a shield, juniors actually accepting it. Whether the people above you honor that compact is, candidly, the biggest single variable in whether this career feels demanding-but-sustainable or simply brutal, and it varies far more by team than by firm. When you evaluate this industry, evaluate that.",
    after: "The cold walk back to the hotel, tired in the eyes, wired in the chest.",
  },
  "management-consultant-dil-d1-b7": {
    before: "Eight minutes of midwestern sidewalk between the war room and your hotel bed. The day replays itself without asking permission.",
    simulatedWork: "Eight minutes of midwestern sidewalk between the war room and your hotel bed, and the day replays itself without asking permission.\n\nFourteen hours ago you walked this same sidewalk the other direction with six draft pages and a plan. Since then you won a storyline argument with one sentence, built the best diagram of your short career, had a machine catch your two-message page and a friend catch your half-million-dollar footnote, watched a partner kill half your morning in nine seconds and understood, eventually, that he was right, and learned the difference between describing work and selling an answer. You also laughed harder at 9:45 PM in a windowless conference room than you have in a month. You turn that mix over as you walk, not sure yet what to make of it.\n\nTomorrow at 3, a CEO and a private equity firm spend forty minutes with the thing your team built, and some number on some page, probably yours, gets questioned, live. The deck is ready. You are mostly sure you are.\n\nYour phone buzzes. Priya, to the channel, 11:04 PM: \"v10 to Dan. Great work today. Sleep.\"\n\nYou stand at the hotel door one extra second in the cold, taking honest inventory of your own reaction to all of it. Somewhere under the exhaustion there is an unmistakable hum. You decide that is worth knowing about yourself, and go inside.",
    commentary: "Compress this day and you hold consulting's honest trade in one frame: real intellectual craft, feedback that is fast and occasionally bruising, hours nobody should romanticize, machine tools quietly absorbing the old grunt work while raising the bar for human judgment, team closeness that alumni miss for decades, and work that important people will actually use to move real money. Some people read this day and feel tired. Some feel a pull. Many honest people feel both at once, and the proportions shift week to week for years, which is why the industry's veterans say you cannot evaluate this career from a brochure, only from texture like this. Whatever you felt on this walk, that feeling is data. This entire simulation exists so you could collect it on a sidewalk in your imagination, for free, instead of three months into a job that charges market rate for the lesson.",
    after: "Lights out at 11:40. Tomorrow: the dry run, the pre-wire, the boardroom, and the question that finds its way to you. But that is the Full Simulation's story. Today was the work behind the moment, and now you know what it costs, and what it gives back, and roughly which way your own scale tips.",
  },
  "management-consultant-full-d0-b1": {
    before: "Sunday evening, your apartment. The weekend was real: you slept in Saturday, saw college friends, went for a long run, and did not open the laptop once. But Sunday after dinner, the week starts pulling at you, the way it does for almost everyone in this industry. Consultants have a dozen names for the feeling. None of them are affectionate.",
    simulatedWork: "The ritual takes shape around 8 PM, half logistics, half mental gear-shift, and you notice you do it now without deciding to. The suitcase first. By week 3 it practically packs itself: four days of client-appropriate clothes, the toiletry kit that never gets unpacked, chargers for both phones. Carry-on, always. Checking a bag on a Monday 6 AM flight is a rookie move that costs you twenty minutes on the far end, and Marcus mocked you out of it in week 1.\n\nThen the calendar scan, and you feel the week start to pull. Monday: 6:05 flight, war room at 8:30, team problem-solving at 1. Thursday sits in bold: STEERCO, 3:00 PM. The whole week is shaped like a funnel pointing at that one meeting.\n\nAt 9:40 your work phone buzzes. Priya, to the channel: a payroll file from Fresca and one line, \"Worth a look before tomorrow. Nothing required tonight.\" You notice the second sentence and are quietly grateful for it; not every manager writes it. You skim the column headers for ten minutes anyway, because some part of your brain has already flown to Columbus, then make yourself close the laptop. Lights out at 10:45. Alarm: 4:50.",
    commentary: "The Sunday-night feeling is one of the most universal and least advertised parts of this career, and it is worth being honest about: when your week starts with a 6 AM flight and a deliverable funnel, Sunday evenings stop being fully yours. How heavy that feels varies enormously by person and by team. Notice the small things that make it livable here: a packing routine that runs on autopilot, and a manager who marks messages \"nothing required tonight\" so a Sunday ping does not become Sunday work. Teams at most major firms now set explicit norms about this, when email is expected, which nights are protected, because the firms learned the hard way that the alternative burns people out. When you evaluate a consulting offer someday, the quality of those norms on your actual team will shape your life more than anything in the offer letter.",
    after: "Sleep comes slower than it should. Tomorrow, the Monday migration: the flight, the war room, and 1.2 million rows of payroll data with a question hiding somewhere inside them.",
  },
  "management-consultant-full-d1-b1": {
    before: "The 4:50 alarm, the dark Uber, the airport. It is week 3, and the Monday routine is calcifying into muscle memory. Your flight to Columbus boards at 6:05. Half the gate area looks exactly like you: quarter-zips, roller bags, laptops out, the great Monday-morning migration of America's consultants to their client cities.",
    simulatedWork: "Security is fast. You have airline status now, a strange artifact of this job: three months in, you have flown more than in your entire previous life, and the loyalty programs have noticed. The second-years talk about the points like a currency, vacations paid entirely in miles, the one part of the travel grind everyone openly defends.\n\nAt the gate you find Marcus and claim the seat beside him. The unwritten team rule, imported from Priya's first firm and now law: no work talk before wheels-up. So you spend boarding arguing about whether a hot dog is a sandwich, which gets genuinely heated and which you are not sure you win.\n\nIn the air, laptop open, you take a first pass at the payroll file, not building anything on hostile wifi, just orienting. 240 stores, two years of weekly data, hours and wages by job code. You sketch a decomposition on the notepad: labor cost is hours times rates. If labor as a percent of sales is up 2.1 points, the culprit is wages rising, hours rising, or sales falling against sticky labor. Three doors. By the time the wheels touch down you know which one you'll open first.\n\nThen the landing ritual: phone off airplane mode, the buzz of accumulated messages, a text from your mom (\"Good luck this week!\") sandwiched between two client emails and one from Jake at the sponsor that you decide, on instinct, Priya should see before you touch it.",
    commentary: "Welcome to the rhythm that defines this industry's lifestyle: out Monday, home Thursday, the same flight numbers week after week until the gate agents start to recognize you. A few honest notes. The status-and-points economy is real and genuinely beloved, the closest thing consulting has to a universal perk. The two-phone, always-reachable texture is also real, and how intrusive it feels depends heavily on your manager's norms. And notice the \"no work talk before wheels-up\" rule: the best teams build small rituals that protect the human relationships inside the machine, because those four people are about to spend more waking hours together than with anyone else in their lives. One more thing: travel intensity varies more than it used to. Since the pandemic, some engagements run three days on site, some are mostly remote with monthly visits, and some, like a PE-owned operations study, still run the full classic rhythm. You do not always get to choose.",
    after: "Rental car, coffee, the drive to Fresca headquarters with Marcus. War room check-in at 8:30. The week officially begins.",
  },
  "management-consultant-full-d1-b2": {
    before: "The \"war room\" is a windowless conference room at Fresca HQ that the client surrendered to your team for the duration of the study. Whiteboard walls, a temperamental printer, a box of granola bars Priya expensed in week 1, and a hand-drawn sign Marcus made that says DAYS WITHOUT A FORMATTING INCIDENT: 0. Monday morning starts here, every week, with Priya at the whiteboard.",
    simulatedWork: "[Scene: the war room, a windowless conference room Fresca surrendered to the team. Whiteboard walls, a temperamental printer, a hand-drawn sign Marcus made reading DAYS WITHOUT A FORMATTING INCIDENT: 0. Priya is already at the board. She draws a horizontal line and writes THURS 3PM: STEERCO at the far right end.]\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): Everything this week exists to make that meeting good. Dan presents. The audience is the CEO, the CFO, and Jake plus a Managing Director from the sponsor. Our story has three chapters: where the margin went, what we found, and the path to the thirty million in their value-creation plan. Marcus, pricing is chapter two. Labor is also chapter two.\n\n[She turns to you.]\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): Where are we on payroll?\n\nAnalyst (Consulting): Got the file last night, first pass on the plane. Working hypotheses: wage inflation, hours creep, or overstaffing. Don't know which yet.\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): I want a point of view by the 1 PM problem-solving session. Not a perfect answer. A point of view.\n\n[She writes the week's deadlines backward from Thursday: draft pages Wednesday morning, Dan review Wednesday 2 PM, final deck Wednesday night, dry run Thursday 7:30. Then she taps the marker on the board, and her tone shifts.]\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): Housekeeping. Jake emailed Dan over the weekend asking for a preview of the labor finding. We do not preview half-baked findings to the sponsor. That is how teams die. All sponsor communication goes through me or Dan this week. Clear?\n\nAnalyst (Consulting): Clear.\n\n[The meeting takes forty minutes. Nobody checks a phone the entire time.]",
    commentary: "This is how consulting teams plan: backward from the moment of truth, never forward from today. Two other things to notice. First, \"a point of view, not a perfect answer.\" Consulting runs on forming an answer early and improving it under attack, the reverse of how school taught you to work, and it is the adjustment new hires say takes longest. Second, watch Priya manage the sponsor. Private equity clients run hot: they move fast, ask sharp questions at all hours, and reward speed. An unmanaged sponsor will happily pull half-finished analysis out of a nervous associate and then anchor on it forever. Part of an engagement manager's job is being the membrane between the team and that pressure. When you see a manager say \"all communication goes through me,\" that is not ego. That is armor, and it is protecting you.",
    after: "You set up at your corner of the war room: laptop, borrowed second monitor, headphones. Three and a half hours to turn 240 stores of payroll data into a point of view.",
  },
  "management-consultant-full-d1-b3": {
    before: "Time to earn your salary. The payroll file has roughly 1.2 million rows: every store, every week, every job code, two years deep. The question sounds simple: labor as a percent of sales is up 2.1 points year over year. Why?",
    simulatedWork: "Time to earn your salary. The payroll file has roughly 1.2 million rows, every store, every week, every job code, two years deep, and the question sounds deceptively simple: labor as a percent of sales is up 2.1 points year over year. Why?\n\nYou start the way you sketched on the plane, decompose. You build the bridge in Excel. Wage rates: up 4 percent, real but explaining less than half the gap. The rest is hours. You cut hours by job code, regular hours basically flat, overtime up 23 percent year over year. There is door number one.\n\nBefore going deeper you do the thing that has quietly become a reflex: you ask the firm's internal AI assistant whether the firm has done restaurant labor work before. It surfaces two sanitized prior studies and a benchmark range in about forty seconds, quick-service labor typically runs 25 to 30 percent of sales, scheduling-driven overtime is a known failure mode. Twenty minutes of reading saves you from reinventing three wheels. What it cannot tell you is anything about Fresca. That part is still yours.\n\nSo you cut Fresca's overtime by store, and the distribution stops you cold. It is not 240 stores drifting up together. Roughly 40 stores account for over 60 percent of the entire overtime increase, and the other 200 look normal. You build a quick chart, stores ranked by overtime growth, and the cliff in it is so sharp it looks like a data error. You check it twice. It is not an error. You Slack Priya: \"Have something. OT is the story, and it's concentrated. ~40 stores driving most of it.\" She replies in ninety seconds: \"Interesting. Bring it at 1.\"",
    commentary: "This block shows you the honest modern shape of analyst work. Yes, you will live in spreadsheets for a meaningful share of your first two years. But the work is changing fast: every major firm now has internal AI tools that consultants use constantly, for prior-work search, research synthesis, first-draft slides, even automated formatting review, and they genuinely save hours of what used to be junior grunt work. Surveys and internal studies at the big firms suggest these tools cut research-and-synthesis time substantially, and most junior consultants use them weekly or daily. What the tools have not replaced, and this matters for your career planning, is exactly what this associate just did: choosing the right decomposition, knowing which cut of the data to run, and recognizing that a cliff in a chart means something. The industry's own quiet anxiety is what AI does to the bottom of its pyramid. The defensible skill is judgment, and this week is one long lesson in how judgment gets built.",
    after: "You grab a sandwich from Fresca's test kitchen downstairs (a genuine perk of restaurant clients) and build three quick slides for the 1 PM session. You feel good. Possibly too good.",
  },
  "management-consultant-full-d1-b4": {
    before: "The 1 PM problem-solving session is a consulting institution: the team in a room, no client, pressure-testing each other's work before any of it goes near someone important. You are presenting the overtime finding. You have three slides and a chart you are quietly proud of.",
    simulatedWork: "[Scene: a small team room, no client present. You stand at the screen with three slides and a chart you are quietly proud of. Priya and Marcus sit across from you.]\n\nAnalyst (Consulting): So, the labor bridge. Overtime up twenty-three percent year over year, and it's not chain-wide. Forty stores drive more than sixty percent of the increase. Here's the cliff.\n\n[Marcus actually says \"huh,\" which from him is a standing ovation. Priya studies the chart in silence for a long moment.]\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): What changed in those forty stores?\n\nAnalyst (Consulting): That's my next step. Could be management, could be scheduling practice.\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): Here's my worry. Didn't Fresca extend breakfast hours in a few hundred locations this spring?\n\n[The room goes quiet. You feel where she's going before she finishes.]\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): If your forty stores are some of the ones that expanded operating hours, then more overtime might just be more open hours. Your chart would look exactly like this and mean nothing.\n\nAnalyst (Consulting): (after a beat) I didn't control for operating hours. So the finding might be real, or it might be mix.\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): Right. And we cannot put a maybe in front of this sponsor. Jake will pull one loose thread and unravel your whole section in front of the CEO, politely, while smiling. Normalize for operating hours and see what survives. End of day if you can.\n\n[On the way out, Marcus bumps your shoulder.]\n\nMarcus (Associate): She did the exact same thing to my pricing curve in week 1. It's a rite of passage. Means she thinks the work is worth fixing.",
    commentary: "Watch what happened, because this is the most important block of the day. The associate got caught with a hole in the analysis, in front of the team, three hours after feeling brilliant. This is not failure; this is the system functioning. The entire point of problem-solving sessions is to find the hole in this room instead of Thursday in the boardroom. The best consultants develop an almost strange relationship with being wrong: they hunt their own holes, because every one found early is an embarrassment avoided later. Notice also the social mechanics: Priya attacked the analysis, never the analyst, and Marcus translated the experience afterward. Veterans of this industry consistently say the feedback intensity, daily, direct, relentless, is the thing that develops people fastest AND the thing that breaks people who hear every critique as a verdict on themselves. Knowing which of those reactions lives in you is worth more than any case-prep book.",
    after: "Back to your corner. The afternoon just wrote its own plan: rebuild with operating hours in it, and learn whether the morning's finding is real or a mirage.",
  },
  "management-consultant-full-d1-b5": {
    before: "You need store-level operating hours, which are not in the payroll file. Twenty minutes of digging in the engagement data room turns up a store master file, updated quarterly, with hours of operation by location. It will do.",
    simulatedWork: "You need store-level operating hours, which are not in the payroll file. Twenty minutes of digging in the engagement data room turns up a store master file, updated quarterly, with hours of operation by location. It will do. You join the files, compute overtime per operating hour, and rerun everything. This is the moment of truth for the morning's work, and you genuinely do not know which way it breaks.\n\nThe answer: the finding shrinks, but survives. About a third of the spike in your 40 stores was the breakfast-hours mirage, exactly what Priya suspected. The rest is real, even normalized, those stores run materially more overtime per operating hour than comparable locations. Something is happening in how they schedule people.\n\nMid-rebuild you hit a wall joining two files and Slack Marcus: \"store_id vs location_code, getting orphan rows, help.\" He answers inside a minute with a screenshot: \"Mapping tab hidden in the data dictionary. Took me a week to find. Welcome to Fresca.\" Then, a beat later: \"Also the IT guy who owns the payroll feed is named Gary and he answers exactly one email a day, so batch your asks.\"\n\nBy 6:15 you have a clean normalized cut and a humbler, truer cliff chart. You message Priya: \"Finding survives normalization. Smaller but real. ~40 stores, OT per operating hour well above peers. Cause unknown.\" She replies: \"Good. Cause is this week's job. Dinner 7:30, Dan's joining. Also: nice turnaround.\"",
    commentary: "Two lessons live in this block. First, the finding shrank when tested, and that is the normal physics of analysis: almost nothing survives first contact at full size, which is why experienced consultants hold conclusions loosely until they have been attacked from several angles. Second, notice the tribal knowledge: the hidden mapping tab, the one-email-a-day IT contact. Every engagement runs on unwritten knowledge that lives in people rather than documents, and the associates who thrive are shameless about asking and generous about answering. There is a myth that consulting rewards lone genius. The reality, repeated by practically everyone who has done the job, is that it rewards people who build a web of allies fast, on the team, at the client, even in the IT department. Nobody bills a single hour alone.",
    after: "You pack up and walk the eight minutes to the hotel, the same mid-tier business hotel as every week, where the front desk staff now greet the team by name. Drop the bag, splash water on your face, downstairs by 7:25. Dinner with a partner is not a test. It is also not nothing.",
  },
  "management-consultant-full-d1-b6": {
    before: "Team dinner at the Italian place near the hotel, the one the team has been to enough times that the waiter no longer brings menus for Priya. Dan flew in this afternoon for tomorrow's client meetings.",
    simulatedWork: "[Scene: the Italian place near the hotel, where the waiter no longer brings Priya a menu. Dan flew in this afternoon for tomorrow's client meetings. For the first hour, nobody mentions Fresca.]\n\nDan (Partner): I had a CEO once stop a presentation on page two and make me defend a single footnote for forty minutes. (beat) Best forty minutes of diligence we ever did, in hindsight.\n\n[Priya and Marcus relitigate a months-old argument about the best airport in America. Then, over coffee, Dan turns to you. Casual tone, direct eyes.]\n\nDan (Partner): So. Labor. What do we know?\n\n[You give him the sixty-second version, almost without planning to: overtime, concentrated in forty stores, survives normalization for operating hours, cause unknown but you suspect scheduling practice rather than wages. Dan nods slowly.]\n\nDan (Partner): Good. Concentrated problems have concentrated fixes, and the sponsor will love that. Their whole value-creation plan is built on findable, fixable money. Get me the cause by Wednesday. Be in stores tomorrow.\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): Already booked. Store visit at eight.\n\n[Back in your room by 9:15, the evening tail: forty-five minutes of email and Slack triage, confirm tomorrow's logistics, flag Jake's weekend email for Priya, decline a calendar invite that collides with the store visit. You call it at 10:00, watch fifteen minutes of basketball highlights as an act of self-respect, and are asleep by 10:45.]",
    commentary: "Two truths share this block. The first is the camaraderie, and it is not recruiting propaganda: ask people who left consulting years ago what they miss and the most common answer is the teams, the dinners, the foxhole closeness of people doing hard things together on the road. The second is quieter: notice that a partner asked a work question over coffee and the associate was expected to have a crisp answer, and notice the forty-five minutes of email that ended the day. The line between social and professional is thin in this world, and the day has a long tail. Most weeks, evenings like this one, done by 10, are the norm and the job is demanding but livable. The weeks where it is not, you will meet on Wednesday.",
    after: "Day one: a thread found, a hole survived, a rebuild that made the work truer, and a partner briefed over tiramisu. Tomorrow you leave the spreadsheet and go watch the overtime happen in person.",
  },
  "management-consultant-full-d2-b1": {
    before: "You and Marcus are driving 25 minutes to a Fresca location in Westerville, one of your 40 high-overtime stores. The plan: watch the morning rush, talk to the General Manager, see the store actually run. Marcus has done a dozen field visits across his engagements. You have done zero, and he knows it, which is why he volunteered to drive.",
    simulatedWork: "[Scene: Marcus drives the 25 minutes to a Fresca store in Westerville, one of your 40. You hold the coffees. You have done zero field visits. He knows it, which is why he volunteered to drive.]\n\nMarcus (Associate): Field trip rules. One: you are a guest in someone's house. That GM runs a real business with real people, and a twenty-four-year-old with a notebook reads as corporate sending spies. There are people at every client who think we're the grim reaper's advance team. Be a human first.\n\nMarcus (Associate): Two: watch the floor more than you talk. Where do lines form? When does the manager personally jump on a station? That's your overtime being born, live, in front of you.\n\nMarcus (Associate): Three: never promise anything. Not \"I'll flag that to corporate,\" nothing. We observe, we thank, we leave.\n\nAnalyst (Consulting): What do I actually ask her?\n\nMarcus (Associate): Open questions, then silence. \"Walk me through how you build the weekly schedule,\" then shut up for two full minutes. The gold is always in the tangents. Go in with a checklist, you get checklist answers.\n\n[He merges onto the highway.]\n\nMarcus (Associate): Also, watch their hands when they talk about the scheduling system. People get physical about software they hate.\n\n[You laugh. He doesn't.]\n\nMarcus (Associate): I'm serious. You'll see.",
    commentary: "This drive is consulting's apprenticeship model in miniature: almost nothing important in this job is taught in training, it is absorbed from someone slightly senior in cars, hallways, and war rooms at odd hours. Marcus is maybe twenty months ahead of you, which in consulting time makes him a veteran, and the transfer happening in this car, how to enter an operator's world without triggering its immune system, is a skill that applies to every field-research situation in any career. One more honest note tucked into his joke about the grim reaper: consultants are not always welcome. At a PE-owned company especially, some employees assume the study ends in cost cuts that have names attached. You will feel that suspicion sometimes. The only honest response is the one Marcus described: respect, curiosity, and never writing checks you cannot cash.",
    after: "You pull into the lot at 7:55. Through the glass you can see the breakfast line already six deep and growing. Time to watch the data turn into people.",
  },
  "management-consultant-full-d2-b2": {
    before: "The Westerville store does about $2.8M a year, top quartile for the chain. The GM, Teresa, has run it for six years. On paper, this store ran 31 percent more overtime per operating hour last quarter than comparable locations. You are here to learn why in person.",
    simulatedWork: "[Scene: the Westerville store, about $2.8M a year, top quartile. The GM, Teresa, has run it six years. She meets you at the counter, friendly and visibly slammed.]\n\nTeresa (General Manager): Corporate said you'd come. You picked a good one. I'm down two people today.\n\n[You take a corner table and watch the 8:15 rush arrive. The bottleneck announces itself within minutes: the digital order station. Mobile orders print in a continuous ribbon, and the one person on them falls behind, then further behind. At 8:40 Teresa herself jumps on the line and starts wrapping burritos. You write down the time. Marcus, without looking up from his coffee, murmurs: \"There it is.\" When the rush breaks, Teresa gives you twenty minutes in the back office. You use the move.]\n\nAnalyst (Consulting): Walk me through how you build the weekly schedule.\n\n[Then you shut up. She laughs without much humor and turns her monitor toward you.]\n\nTeresa (General Manager): I'll show you what corporate thinks my week looks like. The system forecasts my labor from register sales, in fifteen-minute blocks. Very scientific. Except forty percent of my volume is mobile and third-party now, and the forecast barely sees it. If I staff to what the system says, I drown by 8:30 and my service times blow up. So I do what every GM in this region does.\n\n[She pauses, deciding about you.]\n\nTeresa (General Manager): We adjust the inputs until the system spits out the staffing we actually need. And when that's still not enough, I hold people past their shifts. Time and a half. That's your overtime. I'd rather eat the OT than lose the lunch crowd to the place across the street, because nobody at corporate measures the sales I save. They only measure the labor I spend.\n\n[You write the last sentence down word for word. On the wall behind her: a faded printout of the chain's labor targets, and beside it, the crew schedule, covered in handwritten corrections.]",
    commentary: "This is the most valuable hour of the week, and notice that it did not happen in a spreadsheet. The data told you WHERE (40 stores, overtime); only the field could tell you WHY (a forecast that went blind when the business went mobile, and rational managers gaming it to survive). This pattern, systems lagging a changed business, humans absorbing the gap, the absorption showing up as a cost spike somewhere else, is one of the most common findings in all of operations consulting, and the handwritten corrections taped to the wall are its universal symptom. Notice also what unlocked Teresa: open questions, silence, and the fact that you watched her work the line before asking her anything. Operators tell the truth to people who respect the work. One more detail for your toolkit: her line about what gets measured. When an operator says \"nobody measures the thing I'm protecting,\" write it down verbatim. It is almost always the headline of a slide the client has needed for years.",
    after: "You thank Teresa, buy your breakfast bowl on your own card (never expense food from the client's own restaurant, per Marcus, and never take it free), and head out. Next stop: a conference room interview with Carla, the regional manager who oversees 19 of your 40 problem stores.",
  },
  "management-consultant-full-d2-b3": {
    before: "Carla is a Regional Manager with 19 stores, most of them on your list. Regional managers at Fresca are bonused partly on hitting labor cost targets. You are a consultant hired by the private equity firm that just bought her company, three weeks into a study whose stated purpose is finding $30M. She has every reason to treat you as a threat, and for the first fifteen minutes, she does.",
    simulatedWork: "[Scene: a conference room. Carla, Regional Manager over 19 of your 40 stores, bonused partly on hitting labor targets. She arrives exactly on time with a binder she never opens. The first fifteen minutes are smooth and useless, every question meeting a laminated answer.]\n\nCarla (Regional Manager): My stores hit their labor targets. We follow the corporate scheduling process. Overtime is within policy.\n\n[She checks her watch. Twice. You can feel the wall, and you can feel yourself about to push harder, which instinct says is exactly wrong. So you put your pen down.]\n\nAnalyst (Consulting): Carla, I was in Westerville this morning. I watched Teresa jump on the line at 8:40 because the forecast staffed her for register sales and her volume is forty percent mobile now. Nobody is in trouble here. I'm trying to figure out whether the scheduling system gives your managers a fair fight.\n\n[A pause. Her shoulders drop maybe an inch.]\n\nCarla (Regional Manager): The system was built for how we sold food six years ago. My GMs make it work anyway. I would rather a manager hold someone an extra hour than lose a customer to a forty-minute line, because the lost customer never shows up in a report. And before you ask: yes, I know what the overtime costs. I also know three of my stores are in cities with predictive-scheduling laws now, so I can't just slash and rebuild schedules last-minute even if I wanted to. The tool doesn't know that either. My GMs hold people over because it's the only legal lever left that works.\n\n[It is not everything, but it is honest, and it tells you Westerville is no exception. You ask two more open questions and mostly listen. As she leaves, she stops at the door.]\n\nCarla (Regional Manager): Teresa said you actually watched the line before you asked her anything. That's the only reason I told you any of this.",
    commentary: "A defensive interview is a rite of passage, and remember the mechanics of what unlocked it: the associate stopped sounding like an audit, showed firsthand respect for the operator's reality, and explicitly separated the system from the people. Defensiveness in interviews is almost never about hiding laziness; it is about incentives. Carla is bonused on the very number you are investigating, so your questions sound like questions about her paycheck. When someone goes defensive, pushing harder hardens the wall; making honesty safe dissolves it. Notice also the substance she added: compliance constraints (several cities now have fair-workweek laws restricting last-minute schedule changes) that make her managers' workaround the rational move. Real operating problems are almost always overdetermined like this, a system gap, an incentive gap, and a legal constraint braided together, and the consultants clients trust are the ones who come back from the field knowing the whole braid, not just the spreadsheet strand.",
    after: "You walk out with the real story taking shape: a forecast blind to mobile volume, managers rationally gaming it, compliance rules removing the easy fixes, and overtime as the visible symptom. Lunch at the war room desk. This afternoon, the notes become an argument.",
  },
  "management-consultant-full-d2-b4": {
    before: "You have a notebook full of field notes, a normalized dataset, and a hypothesis that finally has a mechanism. Now comes the skill nobody teaches in school: synthesis. Compressing everything you know into one argument an executive can absorb in thirty seconds.",
    simulatedWork: "Back at the war room desk, you face the skill nobody teaches in school: compressing everything you saw into one sentence an executive can absorb in thirty seconds. You open a blank page and force yourself to write the finding. First attempt: \"Overtime analysis of high-OT stores and field interviews suggest scheduling system limitations.\" You read it back. It is mush. No verb doing any work, no \"so what,\" no money.\n\nYou try again, this time as if explaining to a smart friend who has thirty seconds: \"Fresca's labor forecast can't see mobile orders, so managers in high-volume stores game the inputs and burn overtime to protect service. Fix the forecast and most of the $9M overtime problem fixes itself.\" Better. A villain (the blind forecast), a mechanism (rational gaming), a prize (the $9M).\n\nYou build the pyramid beneath it, then rough the exhibits: the cliff chart, the forecast-gap diagram, a sizing build. For the sizing, you ask the internal AI assistant to pressure-test your math against yesterday's benchmarks. It flags that your implied sales-per-labor-hour in the 40 stores would be implausibly low if all the excess overtime were pure waste, which sharpens your wording from \"eliminate\" to \"most of.\" A machine just saved you from an overclaim, and you note it with something between gratitude and unease. By 4:00 you have a one-page storyline and the skeleton of your SteerCo section.",
    commentary: "What you just watched is the pyramid principle, the communication discipline that defines this industry: lead with the answer, support beneath, never the reverse. The test of a headline is whether it would survive being the only sentence the CEO reads, because some Thursdays it is. Notice the rewrite loop, the first draft of every \"so what\" is mush, and the skill is refusing to accept your own mush. And notice the AI moment, because it is the honest 2026 texture of this job: the assistant checks math, surfaces benchmarks, and catches overclaims, while the associate still owns the argument, the field evidence, and the judgment about what it all means. Junior consultants today describe exactly this ambivalence, the tools make you faster and better while quietly raising the bar for what only humans add. The ones who thrive treat the tool as a tireless junior teammate and reserve their own hours for the parts it cannot do: the store floor, the defensive interview, the choice of the one sentence that matters.",
    after: "Priya pinged earlier: 4 PM check-in on the field day. You print the one-pager. Time to find out if the story stands.",
  },
  "management-consultant-full-d2-b5": {
    before: "A 4 PM check-in with Priya in the war room: the store visit, the Carla interview, your one-page storyline. You think the day went well. You are about to learn what a good manager does with \"went well.\"",
    simulatedWork: "[Scene: 4 PM, the war room. You hand Priya the one-pager, the store notes, the Carla interview. You think the day went well. Priya reads the one-pager in silence, then your notes, then circles Teresa's line about what gets measured.]\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): This is good. The mechanism is right, the storyline is clean, and this quote is the emotional center of your section. Now: what didn't you ask?\n\n[You wait, because you have learned she is not being rhetorical.]\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): Turnover. If those forty stores are gaming schedules and holding crews late every day, what is that doing to quits? This industry already loses most of its hourly crew every year, one of the highest-turnover sectors in the economy. If your stores are worse than the chain's own baseline, your nine million is understated, because every replaced crew member costs real money to hire and train. And when Jake asks \"what else does this break?\" on Thursday, I want us holding the answer already.\n\n[She is right, and the question now seems obvious, which is the worst kind of miss. She reads your face with slightly annoying accuracy.]\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): Don't spiral, you got the hard part today. Pull turnover by store from the HR feed tonight, it's a fast cut. Schedule two more regional calls for Thursday or Friday. And one wording note: your headline says \"most of the $9M.\" By Thursday, be precise. \"Most\" is an invitation, and Jake accepts all invitations.",
    commentary: "Feel the texture of this feedback: immediate, specific, aimed at the work, and delivered with the assumption you can take it. Consulting runs the fastest feedback loops in white-collar work, daily and sometimes hourly, and this is simultaneously why the job develops people at an absurd rate and why people who need praise-wrapped feedback struggle in it. But watch what Priya is actually installing in you: anticipation. The gap between a good analysis and a great one is that the great one has already answered the question the room has not asked yet. That instinct gets built exactly this way, one missed question at a time, named out loud, in a safe room, by someone whose job description quietly includes making you better. Also file away her industry fact: hourly turnover in fast casual routinely exceeds 70 percent a year. When your finding touches a number like that, the prize compounds.",
    after: "Two to-dos and one bruise that you have already decided is a teaching bruise. The evening: turnover pull, then turning the storyline into actual draft pages, because tomorrow morning the deck becomes real.",
  },
  "management-consultant-full-d2-b6": {
    before: "Draft pages for your section are due into the team deck before tomorrow's 8 AM huddle. With the storyline set, the pages almost design themselves; the discipline tonight is \"ghosting,\" sketching every page with its headline and rough layout before perfecting any single one.",
    simulatedWork: "You do the turnover pull first, because Priya's instincts have earned the right to go first. The HR feed gives it up in forty minutes: crew turnover in your 40 stores runs roughly 20 points above the chain's own average, on a baseline that is already brutal because this is fast casual. The overworked stores are bleeding people, exactly as she suspected. Your section just grew a second engine.\n\nThen you ghost the pages, six of them: a section header, the cliff chart, the forecast-gap mechanism, the field-evidence page built around Teresa's measured-versus-saved line (anonymized), the sizing build now including turnover cost, and a placeholder for \"what we'd do about it\" that Priya and Dan will shape. Each gets a full-sentence headline and a sketched exhibit, and you make yourself resist perfecting the sizing chart's formatting. Shape first, polish later. Pages die in review, and polishing a corpse is wasted heartbreak.\n\nAt 6:30 you hit the wall where you read the same sentence three times, so you do the thing the second-years all preach: thirty minutes in the sad little hotel gym, shower, a salad in the lobby restaurant half-watching a basketball game. A text from your college roommate: \"you alive?\" You send back a photo of the salad: \"Thriving, clearly.\" Back up by 7:45, weirdly restored, and the last pages take forty minutes. At 8:30 you post the file: \"Six ghost pages in the folder for the huddle. Turnover in: ~20pts above chain avg in our 40. Sizing updated.\" Priya, two minutes later: \"Saw the turnover number. Excellent. Sleep.\"",
    commentary: "Two durable lessons. First, ghosting: professionals design the whole story cheaply before perfecting any part, because tomorrow a partner may kill a third of these pages and the only question is how much polishing you will have wasted on the dead. Second, the gym break and the roommate text are not filler, they are the actual content of this block. The honest shape of consulting hours is this: most travel-week days run roughly nine to seven plus an evening tail, heavy but livable, with periodic spikes you will meet tomorrow. The people who last learn energy management young, real food, some movement, guarded sleep, a life outside the job kept deliberately alive, and the people who flame out are usually the ones who treated every week like a sprint until their bodies unionized. There is an old line in the industry that everyone is only ever two bad projects away from quitting. The dark version is true for some people. The useful version is: how you run your evenings is a vote on which kind of consultant you become.",
    after: "Day two closes with field evidence, a storyline, a second engine for your section, and six ghost pages in the folder. Tomorrow is the hardest day of the week: build morning, partner review at 2, and whatever the review breaks, the night must fix. Sleep while you can.",
  },
  "management-consultant-full-d3-b1": {
    before: "Deck day. The SteerCo presentation gets built today, reviewed by Dan at 2 PM, and finalized tonight. The morning huddle is where Priya assembles three workstreams into one storyline. On the whiteboard when you walk in: a numbered outline with dashes under each number. Consultants call it a dot-dash, and the deck gets written this way, as headlines, before a single slide exists.",
    simulatedWork: "[Scene: the war room, deck day. Priya at the whiteboard, a numbered dot-dash outline already up. You and Marcus at the table.]\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): Twenty-five pages, three chapters. Chapter one, the margin bridge, where the 2.1 points went. Chapter two, the two engines, pricing and labor. Chapter three, the path to the thirty million and the week 4-to-8 plan. Jake's MD is flying in for this, so assume the toughest version of every question.\n\n[She runs the dot-dash at speed. At your section she compresses six pages to five dots, evidence first, then mechanism. You push.]\n\nAnalyst (Consulting): I'd lead with the mechanism. The forecast gap is what makes the forty stores make sense, and it's what Rachel's team gets attacked over if we sequence it wrong.\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): You're right, and the Rachel point is the better reason. Mechanism first.\n\n[She rewrites the dots. Marcus's raised eyebrow across the table is the only acknowledgment you need.]\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): Your five pages plus support on a pricing exhibit, everything to me by 12:30 for assembly before Dan at 2. And protected lunch. Twenty real minutes, away from the laptop. Long day. Start it like an adult.",
    commentary: "The dot-dash is one of the industry's core rituals: debate the argument while it is words on a whiteboard, because changing a headline costs nothing and changing a finished slide costs an afternoon. Notice the pushback that won. A three-month associate disagreed with the manager, attached a reason rooted in the client's politics, and the storyline changed. Good teams genuinely run this way, hierarchy decides, but ideas compete as equals, and the fastest way a junior person earns standing is exactly this: disagreeing rarely, briefly, and with a reason. One more detail worth copying for life: the protected lunch. The teams that survive deck days without fraying have managers who ration the team's energy like it is the scarce resource it actually is.",
    after: "Headphones on, calendar blocked, the war room quiet except for keyboards. Four and a half hours to turn five dots into five pages that can survive Dan.",
  },
  "management-consultant-full-d3-b2": {
    before: "The longest unbroken stretch of craft in the week: ghost pages become finished ones. Real exhibits, checked numbers, headlines that earn their place. The turnover engine from last night gets built in properly.",
    simulatedWork: "This is the longest unbroken stretch of craft in the week, and people who like making things tend to secretly love it. You build in priority order, focus narrowing to the width of the screen.\n\nThe cliff chart first, rebuilt clean: 240 stores ranked by normalized overtime, your 40 shaded, one callout. Headline as a full sentence: \"Overtime is not a chain-wide problem: 40 stores drive over 60% of the increase.\"\n\nThen the page you are quietly proudest of: the forecast-gap diagram, the system seeing register sales on the left, blind to the mobile ribbon on the right, and the chain of human workarounds that follows. No jargon anywhere on it. The bar you hold it to: Rachel's COO peers should grasp it in ten seconds and feel described, not accused.\n\nThe sizing build folds in turnover: the overtime prize plus an estimated $2 to 3M in avoidable churn cost, total roughly $11M. You run the draft pages through the firm AI assistant's slide-review feature, which grades them against best-practice templates. It flags two things: your evidence page has two messages fighting for one headline, and the sizing footnote font has drifted. The first catch is genuinely good, you split the page. The second makes you feel simultaneously grateful and like the machine is your most pedantic coworker.\n\nAt 11:50, Marcus on Slack: \"Trade you a sanity check. My price elasticity page for your turnover math.\" You swap, each find one soft spot, each fix it. At 12:20 five pages go to Priya. Her reply: \"Solid. Assembling. Dan at 2. Be in the room, both of you.\"",
    commentary: "This is the craft block, and people who like making things tend to secretly love it: hours of deep focus where a week of mess becomes pages with one message each, stated as a full-sentence headline, provable from the exhibit beneath. If a page needs you standing next to it to make sense, it fails. Notice two textures of the modern version. The AI slide-reviewer catching a two-message page is exactly the kind of quality backstop these tools now provide inside major firms, and the associate's mixed feelings about it, gratitude plus a flicker of \"what does this mean for people like me\", is the live conversation happening at every junior level in the industry right now. And notice the peer swap with Marcus: the person who built a page is the worst person alive to proofread it, so good teams trade. Quality in consulting is not a final step. It is a habit distributed through the whole day.",
    after: "Protected lunch, as ordered, twenty minutes in the Fresca test kitchen arguing with Marcus about whether the new menu item will survive the quarter. At 2 PM, Dan reviews the deck. You have been told partner reviews are an experience. Time to find out.",
  },
  "management-consultant-full-d3-b3": {
    before: "Partner review. Dan has 90 minutes between client meetings to judge 25 pages that took the team three days to build. Priya projects; you and Marcus sit with notebooks. You have rehearsed nothing, because you are not presenting. It turns out that does not mean you are safe.",
    simulatedWork: "[Scene: the war room. Priya drives the deck; you and Marcus sit with notebooks. Dan stays standing, jacket on, and flips all 25 pages in two minutes, reading only headlines, before back to page one.]\n\nDan (Partner): The story is buried. Page one has to do all the work: we found a credible path to the thirty million, here are the two engines, labor and pricing, here is what we do next. The CEO should walk out after page one and repeat our answer to his board word for word. Right now page one is an agenda, and agendas are for meetings nobody remembers.\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): Agreed. We'll flip it. Answer first.\n\n[He reaches your section.]\n\nDan (Partner): Cliff chart, keep it, best evidence in the deck. The mechanism diagram, move it earlier, it's the best page in here.\n\n[A half-second of relief. Then, no change in tone:]\n\nDan (Partner): Kill these three. The field-evidence quotes, the methodology page, the sizing build. Quotes to appendix, this sponsor distrusts anecdotes on principle. Methodology to appendix, nobody cares how the sausage was made until they doubt the sausage. The sizing becomes one number on the summary page. In a forty-minute meeting every page is rent, and three of yours aren't paying.\n\nAnalyst (Consulting): (it lands under the sternum) Understood.\n\n[Nine seconds, three pages, most of your morning. Priya catches your eye: normal, write it down, keep moving. Dan gathers his jacket.]\n\nDan (Partner): Good deck underneath. Answer-first by tonight.\n\n[At the door, to Priya but looking at you:]\n\nDan (Partner): The overtime number. Who owns it?\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): (nodding at you) He does.\n\nDan (Partner): Know it cold by tomorrow. The MD will go there.",
    commentary: "Every consultant alive remembers their first partner review, usually with a wince and eventually with gratitude, and it is worth decoding what Dan actually did. He was not grading effort. He was editing for an audience of four people and the forty minutes of attention the team gets on Thursday, and against that constraint, pages that answer unasked questions are not neutral, they are rent-takers. The pages he killed were good work; that is precisely why they go to the appendix, where they back every number if anyone doubts the sausage, instead of the recycling bin. Learning to cut work you are proud of without bleeding ego on the floor is one of this job's hardest and most transferable lessons, and people who cannot learn it, who defend pages like territory, plateau early no matter how smart they are. Last: \"know it cold\" from a partner is not a suggestion. It means that tomorrow, in some form, that number will be yours, live, in front of the client.",
    after: "The rebuild list is long and the deadline is tonight. Coffee run, sleeves up. The war room becomes home for the evening, and weirdly, the mood is good: there is a specific energy that arrives when a team knows exactly what to fix.",
  },
  "management-consultant-full-d3-b4": {
    before: "The deck restructures around Dan's edits: a new executive summary page carrying the entire story, your section reordered, three pages demoted to appendix, every headline rewritten to lead with the answer. Priya takes the summary page. You take your section and the appendix. Marcus takes pricing.",
    simulatedWork: "The reordering is mechanical; the headline rewriting is not, and you can feel yourself learning in real time.\n\nOld headline: \"Analysis of overtime drivers across the store network.\" New: \"Fixing the labor forecast unlocks ~$11M with no service impact.\" The first describes work; the second sells an answer. Once you see the difference you cannot unsee it, and you go back through every page hunting description disguised as argument. You find it twice more.\n\nAt 6:30 Priya posts her summary page to the channel: the whole eight-week study in one page, three numbers, three sentences, the kind of simple that is obviously expensive. You say so. Her reply: \"Eighth draft. The first seven were garbage. That is the entire job.\"\n\nAround 7, a ripple: Jake emails Dan asking whether the team can \"walk the sponsor through preliminary findings tonight, just informally.\" You watch the politics resolve in real time on the channel: Dan replies that the team will see them at SteerCo with completed work, warmly, in two sentences, copying no one. Priya posts a single raised-eyebrow emoji. Marcus, deadpan: \"And that, children, is why partners get paid.\"\n\nBy 7:40 your section is rebuilt and your slain pages are settled into the appendix, demoted but load-bearing.",
    commentary: "\"Answer-first\" is the deepest habit this industry will install in you, and it follows people for life, into emails, interviews, and eventually how they think: most professions present evidence and then conclude, consulting concludes and then proves, because executives decide in thirty seconds whether the next thirty are worth having. Hold onto Priya's \"eighth draft\", the polish in final decks is not talent, it is iteration nobody puts on a recruiting poster. And study the Jake maneuver, because it is a masterclass in client management you will not get in training: the sponsor tried to pull unfinished work through a side door, and the partner declined in a way that protected the team, preserved the relationship, and conceded nothing, in under forty words. Knowing what to say is a skill. Knowing what not to send before Thursday is a career.",
    after: "Structurally, the deck is sound. What remains is the final mile, numbers traced, pages aligned, the whole thing read aloud, and the team orders room service to the war room. It is going to be a late one, and the energy in the room is, against all logic, good.",
  },
  "management-consultant-full-d3-b5": {
    before: "Big presentations do not end at \"done,\" they end at \"checked.\" Tonight: every number traced to source, every page footnoted, the deck read start to finish aloud. The filename is already FrescaSteerCo_v7, on its way to v9, and someone has quietly added \"_FINAL\" to a draft as a joke, because in consulting \"FINAL\" in a filename is the industry's most reliable lie.",
    simulatedWork: "[Scene: the war room, late, room-service plates pushed aside. The file is FrescaSteerCo_v7, climbing toward v9, and someone has added \"_FINAL\" to an earlier draft as a joke. Priya reads every headline aloud, no slides, testing whether the story stands stripped of pictures. Two get rewritten where her voice snags.]\n\nMarcus (Associate): (at 9:10, quiet) Hey. Your sizing footnote says nine point two. The model says nine point seven.\n\nAnalyst (Consulting): Version-four fossil.\n\nMarcus (Associate): Yep.\n\n[You fix it, initial the checklist, pay him in the last of the room-service fries.]\n\nMarcus (Associate): That one would've been Thursday's whole meeting.\n\n[He's right. At 9:45 the slap-happy hour lands on schedule.]\n\nMarcus (Associate): In a world... where forecasts cannot see burritos...\n\n[Two minutes, nobody breathes. At 10:30, v9 is done.]\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): Good. Both of you, bed. I'll do the final read and send to Dan tonight. Dry run 7:30.\n\nAnalyst (Consulting): I can stay and help.\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): No. Tomorrow needs you sharp, not loyal. Go.\n\n[Walking out, you glance back: she settles in with the deck and a fresh coffee. You understand something new about what the manager job costs.]",
    commentary: "Here is the honest picture of consulting hours, with no recruiting gloss: this is a 14-plus-hour day, and SteerCo weeks contain one or two of them. Typical weeks run 55 to 65 hours; crunch weeks run more; anyone who tells you otherwise wants something from you. Two things inside the grind deserve your attention. First, the checking culture, numbers traced, initialed, owned, exists because one wrong number in front of a sponsor poisons every right one; precision is not perfectionism here, it is the product. Second, watch the end of the night: the manager sending juniors home while she takes the last shift is one of the unwritten compacts of good consulting teams, seniors spend their seniority as a shield, and someday the shield is supposed to become your job. Whether the people above you honor that compact is, candidly, the single biggest variable in whether this career feels sustainable or brutal, and it varies by team far more than by firm.",
    after: "The cold walk back to the hotel, tired in the eyes and wired in the chest. Day three took the roughest review of your young career and ended with a deck you are proud of and a team you would do it again with. Tomorrow it meets the CEO.",
  },
  "management-consultant-full-d4-b1": {
    before: "SteerCo at 3. The morning opens with a dry run in the war room: Dan rehearses the flow, assigns speaking roles, and the team war-games the room. Printed decks sit in a warm stack by the temperamental printer, which chose violence at 7:10 and was defeated by Marcus and a paperclip.",
    simulatedWork: "[Scene: the war room, 7:30 AM. Printed decks in a warm stack by the printer, which chose violence at 7:10 and was defeated by Marcus and a paperclip. Dan runs the deck like he's already in the boardroom, narrating page by page, fast, no wasted motion. He stops on the overtime pages and looks at you.]\n\nDan (Partner): If the CEO or the sponsor goes into the forty stores, the detail is yours. You were inside them this week. That beats anything I can perform. Under a minute: what you saw, why it's a system problem, what it's worth.\n\n[Then the war-gaming, which is its own theater. Priya plays the skeptical CFO.]\n\nPriya (as CFO): Every consultant who has ever sat in that chair has shown me a big number. Why is yours real?\n\n[Marcus plays Jake's MD with unsettling accuracy, leaning back, pen tapping.]\n\nMarcus (as the MD): Forty stores, same system as the other two hundred. Sounds like forty bad managers. Why shouldn't we just replace them?\n\n[You answer it twice, because the first version comes out defensive, and the honest answer, no, the managers are coping rationally with a tool the business outgrew, is the most important sentence in your section.]\n\nDan (Partner): Better. Now the rules. Phones face down, decks closed until I open them, watch the room more than your notes. If a question lands that we can't answer, the phrase is \"we'll have that for you by Friday,\" said once, calmly, with a date. Never guess in that room. A wrong guess costs us the whole deck.\n\n[He gathers his pages.]\n\nDan (Partner): Last thing. Whatever happens in there, no surprises on our faces. We've seen every number before. Look like it.",
    commentary: "Notice the delegation, because it is not generosity, it is strategy: \"the person who was inside the stores\" carries an authenticity in a boardroom no partner can fake, and Dan knows the moment will land harder from you. But the trust was purchased across the week, the Monday hole taken well, the rebuild, the dinner answer, and that is generally how speaking rights work in this industry: they are earned in private before they are granted in public. Notice also the war-gaming ritual itself. Teams rehearse the hostile versions of questions out loud precisely so the real meeting contains no first drafts; the famous consultant calm in client rooms is manufactured the morning before, line by line. And file away the discipline of \"we'll have that by Friday\": in high-stakes rooms, a confident wrong answer is the most expensive sentence you can say, and the professionals are the ones who learned to love saying \"I don't know, yet, here's when I will.\"",
    after: "Deck final, roles set, your sixty seconds rehearsed to the bone. Next: the pre-wire with Rachel, Fresca's COO, the executive whose scheduling system your section quietly indicts.",
  },
  "management-consultant-full-d4-b2": {
    before: "A \"pre-wire\" is the quiet meeting before the real meeting: walking a key executive through findings privately so nothing in the SteerCo blindsides them in front of their boss. Today's is with Rachel, the COO. The scheduling platform reports up to her. Dan brings Priya and, to your mild terror, you.",
    simulatedWork: "[Scene: a quiet meeting before the real one. Dan walks Rachel, the COO, through the deck privately, so nothing blindsides her in front of her CEO. The scheduling platform reports up to her. He brings Priya and, to your mild terror, you. Rachel listens without expression until the forecast-gap page, then takes off her glasses.]\n\nRachel (COO): So your conclusion is that my scheduling platform is the problem.\n\n[Not hostile. Testing.]\n\nDan (Partner): Our conclusion is that the platform was built for a business that was five percent digital and is now forty. Your managers have been covering the gap by hand for two years. Frankly, the field is doing heroic work with a tool the business outgrew.\n\n[A long pause. Rachel puts her glasses back on.]\n\nRachel (COO): That is a fair description. (turning to you, abruptly) You're the one who was in Westerville. What did Teresa actually say?\n\n[The room tilts toward you. You give her the honest version, the line about what gets measured and what gets lost, anonymized exactly as you wrote it.]\n\nRachel (COO): That sentence has been said in my own meetings. It loses every budget fight, because the lost sales are invisible and the labor line isn't. (she taps the page) Between us: my team flagged this gap eighteen months ago and lost. If your number holds this afternoon, I get that budget. Say it in there the way you both just said it to me, system outgrown, field heroic, and I will back you in the room.\n\n[Walking out, Dan murmurs, to nobody in particular:]\n\nDan (Partner): And THAT is why we pre-wire.",
    commentary: "You just watched two skills that most professionals do not acquire until their thirties, if ever. The first is the pre-wire itself, governed by an iron law of client work: never let a senior executive learn something surprising about their own area in front of their boss. Surprised executives become enemies for the duration of the engagement; pre-wired executives become allies who fight for your finding as if it were theirs, because by 3 PM, it is. The second is Dan's framing under pressure: the same truth, \"your system is broken\", delivered as \"your people are heroes working around a tool the business outgrew.\" Both versions are accurate; one creates a defender, the other a defendant. The frame you choose for a finding is not spin, it is the difference between a recommendation that gets implemented and a deck that gets filed, and the industry's open secret is that plenty of technically brilliant work dies in the filing cabinet for exactly this reason. Notice, finally, that Rachel asked the junior person for the ground truth. Field credibility is a currency, and you just spent it well.",
    after: "Back to the war room, four hours to SteerCo, which is when the sponsor's email arrives, because of course it does.",
  },
  "management-consultant-full-d4-b3": {
    before: "At 12:40, Priya forwards an email with one line of her own on top: \"Need this back to me by 2:15, not to Jake directly.\" The email is from Jake: \"Ahead of this afternoon, can you cut the overtime increase by daypart? Want to see if this is a breakfast problem before we talk about the fix. Also flag what this does to the EBITDA bridge for the 100-day plan. Thanks.\"",
    simulatedWork: "At 12:40, Priya forwards an email from Jake with one line of her own on top: \"Need this back to me by 2:15, not to Jake directly.\" Jake's ask: cut the overtime increase by daypart, see if this is a breakfast problem before the room talks fixes, and flag what it does to the EBITDA bridge for the 100-day plan.\n\nA spike of adrenaline, then the routine you are beginning to own: breathe, scope, plan backward from 2:15. The daypart cut is one pivot away. The EBITDA framing is wording, not new math. The real risk is not difficulty, it is a sloppy error at speed, ninety minutes before the number gets discussed in front of the CEO.\n\nYou build the cut, overtime by daypart, your 40 stores against the rest, and as the chart forms you realize it is quietly perfect: the spike concentrates in the breakfast and lunch peaks, precisely where mobile volume hits hardest. Jake's stress test, whether he meant it to or not, confirms your mechanism. The data is agreeing with the story in front of a witness.\n\nThen protocol: you walk it to Marcus for a sixty-second check, he traces the pivot, ties one total to source, nods. \"Clean. And it makes your story stronger, which is the best possible answer to a hostile question.\" You draft the reply, answer first, one attached page, EBITDA framing in a single sentence, zero hedging, to Priya at 1:50. She forwards it to Jake at 1:58 with one word added: \"Attached.\" Jake at 2:06: \"Very helpful. See you at 3.\" Priya, on the channel: \"Daypart cut goes in the appendix. This is now ammunition, not a threat.\"",
    commentary: "Private equity owners run engagements at a different cadence than corporate clients, and this block is the cadence in miniature: sharp questions on short clocks, framed in the sponsor's native language of EBITDA bridges and 100-day plans, with response speed read, fairly or not, as a proxy for competence. Notice the disciplines inside the scramble, because they are the whole lesson: scope before building, a second set of eyes before anything leaves the building, answer-first in the reply, and, critically, the chain of custody, the analysis went through the manager, not straight to the sponsor, because Priya owns that relationship and the team's exposure on it. Notice also what the associate recognized mid-build: the requested cut strengthened the team's own argument. The best possible outcome of a stress test is evidence that survives it, and recognizing that in real time, the data agreeing with your story under hostile examination, is what \"knowing it cold\" was always going to mean.",
    after: "Reply sent, decks printed, water bottle filled, one final read of your sixty seconds in the elevator reflection. At 2:45 the team walks to the executive floor. You have never been this prepared for a meeting in your life, and your heart rate has not received the memo.",
  },
  "management-consultant-full-d4-b4": {
    before: "The boardroom: Fresca's CEO and CFO, Rachel, Jake and his Managing Director, your team of four. Phones face down. Decks closed until Dan opens them. Your job, per the dry run: track the room, support when called, own the overtime detail if it comes up. The \"if\" is doing heavy lifting and you know it.",
    simulatedWork: "[Scene: the boardroom. Fresca's CEO and CFO, Rachel, Jake and his Managing Director, your team of four. Phones face down. Dan opens with the summary page and does exactly what he coached: the whole story in ninety seconds, three numbers, no throat-clearing. The room leans in at \"a credible path to the thirty million.\" The margin bridge lands. Marcus's pricing draws sharp questions from the MD and survives them. Then the labor section, and on the 40-stores page, the CEO takes off his glasses.]\n\nCEO: Help me with something. Forty stores, same system as the other two hundred. Is this a manager problem or a system problem? Because those are two very different conversations, and one of them is short.\n\n[Dan does not glance at the deck.]\n\nDan (Partner): It's exactly the right question, and rather than answer it from a spreadsheet, I'll hand it to the one person in this room who was inside those stores this week.\n\n[Every head turns. The rehearsed sixty seconds is simply there, the way a drilled fire-escape route is there.]\n\nAnalyst (Consulting): It's a system problem with rational people inside it. Tuesday morning I watched a general manager jump onto the line mid-rush because the forecast staffed her for register sales, and forty percent of her volume is now mobile. Your managers are protecting your customers with overtime because the tool stopped seeing the business two years ago. The stores with the most digital volume game the system hardest, which is why it concentrates in forty locations, and it's why the fix is the forecast, not the people. That's most of the eleven million.\n\n[A beat of silence. The CEO writes something down.]\n\nCEO: That is the most useful thing I've heard this month.\n\n[Rachel picks it up on cue, pre-wired to the syllable.]\n\nRachel (COO): It matches what my field teams have been telling us. I'd support piloting a forecast fix in those stores immediately.\n\n[The MD looks at Jake; Jake taps the daypart page, already open in the appendix.]\n\nJake (Sponsor VP): The daypart cut backs it. We'd want the pilot designed against the 100-day plan.\n\n[The meeting runs twenty minutes long, entirely about weeks 4 through 8, which Priya will later tell you is the only standing ovation consulting ever gives.]",
    commentary: "Hold this block when you weigh this career, because this is the payoff the industry sells, and it is real: three months in, your work just changed what a CEO believes, in the room, out loud, with the people who own the company nodding along. Now study the machinery under the moment. Dan handed you the question deliberately, partners build client trust by visibly trusting their teams, and it works precisely because everything around it was controlled: the dry run drilled your sixty seconds, the pre-wire scripted Rachel's support, the lunchtime scramble pre-armed Jake's evidence, and the answer itself was concrete (one store, one manager, one mechanism), structured (answer, evidence, implication), and brief. Boardrooms do not reward everything you know; they reward the one minute of it the room needed, delivered calmly, and the calm was manufactured at 7:30 this morning. One more honest note: weeks of work compressed into a moment that went well. Sometimes it does not, the number gets challenged and wobbles, the room goes cold, and that risk, that the meeting IS the product, is the pressure this job runs on. Some people find it addictive. Some find it corrosive. Today, it was addictive.",
    after: "Handshakes, a \"strong work, team\" from the CFO, and the strange floating feeling of adrenaline with nowhere left to go. Lobby debrief, then the airport. You fly home tonight.",
  },
  "management-consultant-full-d4-b5": {
    before: "The team regroups in the hotel lobby before splitting for the airport, the fifteen-minute ritual after every significant meeting: what landed, what wobbled, what changes next week. Bags already packed, stacked by the door like evidence of the rhythm.",
    simulatedWork: "[Scene: the hotel lobby, bags stacked by the door, the fifteen-minute ritual after every significant meeting. Dan goes first, fast, coat already half on.]\n\nDan (Partner): Strong meeting. The summary page did its job, pricing held under real pressure, and the overtime answer was the moment of the day. (to you) You earned that question before you answered it. Well done. (no gear change) What was weaker? Page 14 dragged, we nearly lost the MD on it. Priya, simplify before that deck travels anywhere. And we promised a pilot design by next Thursday, which means the real work starts Monday and the celebration is over in roughly four minutes.\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): Pilot scope for ten stores, the forecast vendor conversation Rachel offered to broker, and your two remaining regional interviews are urgent now, not optional. Home office tomorrow, debrief call at 9. Expenses before Monday, please. Finance has started naming names.\n\n[Dan, at the door, pauses.]\n\nDan (Partner): First SteerCo?\n\nAnalyst (Consulting): (nodding) Yeah.\n\nDan (Partner): They get easier. The preparation never does. That part is the job.\n\n[And he's gone, into a black car, onto a different client, before you can decide whether that was encouragement or a warning. You suspect the answer is yes.]",
    commentary: "Notice how brief the celebration was: ninety seconds of genuine praise, then directly to what wobbled and what is owed next, and this is not coldness, it is metabolism. Consulting culture digests wins almost instantly, the next deliverable is always already on the clock, and people experience that either as momentum or as a treadmill, often depending on the week, sometimes depending on the year. The fifteen-minute debrief itself is a habit worth stealing for any career: the best teams review every significant performance while it is fresh, with the same unsentimental honesty in victory as in defeat, because the review IS where the next win gets built. And do not miss the small administrative beat at the end. Expense reports are the job's least glamorous recurring chore, every Thursday flight home in this industry has a cabin full of people doing them, and finance really does start naming names.",
    after: "Uber to the airport with Marcus, who spends the ride lobbying for the window seat he already has. The 7:10 home, the week's expense report as in-flight entertainment, and a thought you keep to yourself: some part of you wants Monday to come.",
  },
  "management-consultant-full-d4-b6": {
    before: "Thursday-night flights home are a consulting institution: half the cabin is consultants in various stages of the same wind-down, expense apps open, out-of-office drafts ready, the week draining out of shoulders at altitude. Your ritual is calcifying too: expenses, inbox to zero, then deliberately nothing.",
    simulatedWork: "Expenses first, over the first drink service: hotel, rental, two team dinners (Dan grabbed the big one, partners always do), Teresa's breakfast bowl correctly self-funded, eleven minutes flat. The app's receipt-scanner misreads the parking stub and you fix it with the resignation of a person who has had this exact fight before. The second-years claim a six-minute personal record exists, and you no longer doubt them.\n\nThen the inbox: file, flag, clear. One note to Monday-you: \"Pilot design, start selection logic from Teresa's store outward.\" Then the laptop closes, and you let the week replay. Four days ago you found a cliff in a spreadsheet at this same altitude. Since then it survived Priya, met Teresa, got past Carla's wall, lost half its pages to Dan, was stress-tested by Jake, and ended up in a CEO's notebook with eleven million dollars attached. You pull up the photo of Monday's whiteboard, THURS 3PM: STEERCO, and feel the strange satisfaction of a funnel fully emptied.\n\nYou text your roommate: \"Home by 10. Long week. Genuinely good week.\" The reply lands on the taxiway: \"pizza's on you, consultant. you said that word like a slur in september btw. growth.\"",
    commentary: "The Thursday flight home is where the lifestyle becomes real rather than theoretical: most weeks of a travel engagement you are gone Monday through Thursday, and that rhythm quietly shapes everything, friendships maintained by text, relationships that need a partner who genuinely does not resent Tuesdays, gym routines rebuilt in hotel basements, the running joke about houseplants. People who have done this job will tell you the travel is the variable that ends more consulting careers than the hours do, and also, in the same breath, that the points paid for their honeymoon and the Thursday-night decompression at 36,000 feet became weirdly sacred. Both are true. The real question the rhythm asks is not \"can you survive it\", most people can, for a while, it is \"what does it cost the rest of your life, and is the trade paying you back yet.\" Watch how this week felt from this seat. That feeling is data, and this entire simulation exists so you can collect it for free.",
    after: "Wheels down at 9:15, home by 10, pizza honored. Tomorrow is Friday: home office, no alarm before 7:30, and the gentler closing rhythm of the week, plus one conversation with Marcus that will turn out to matter more than the SteerCo.",
  },
  "management-consultant-full-d5-b1": {
    before: "Friday is home-office day: your own desk, your own coffee mug, a 9 AM video call instead of a war room, and the low-grade luxury of pants with no structural integrity. The agenda: digest the SteerCo and plan week 4, which now orbits one promise made in that boardroom: a pilot design for the scheduling fix, due Thursday.",
    simulatedWork: "[Scene: Friday, home office, a 9 AM video call instead of a war room. Priya shares a one-page week 4 plan, drafted at an hour you decide not to ask about.]\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): Three workstreams. One: pilot design, ten stores. That's yours. (no question mark anywhere near it) Selection logic, the intervention, the measures, the read period. Two: Marcus owns the pricing deep-dive the CFO asked for. Three: I take the vendor conversation Rachel is brokering. Jake has already emailed twice this morning, both about the pilot. The sponsor smells a quick win for the 100-day plan, which is good for us and dangerous for us. A sloppy pilot that fails takes the whole recommendation down, and forty general managers hear about it by lunch. Design it like it has to survive a skeptic, because it has to survive several, and one of them bills by the hour.\n\n[You sketch the shape live on the call: ten stores split between high-overtime and normal locations, a manual forecast adjustment so no software build blocks the start, overtime per operating hour and service times as paired measures so nobody can claim the fix saved money by wrecking service, a four-week minimum read.]\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): Good skeleton. Draft framework to me Tuesday. And put crew turnover in the measures. If the fix works, those stores should stop bleeding people, and that's the slide that makes Rachel's budget case for her.",
    commentary: "Notice the cadence: Thursday's triumph became Friday's to-do list in under eighteen hours, and a three-month associate now owns the design of an experiment a CEO is waiting on, with the sponsor's 100-day plan as the deadline pressure. Responsibility in consulting arrives exactly like this, suddenly, slightly before you feel ready, with no question mark, and that is the design, not an accident: the firm's bet is that ownership under support is the fastest teacher ever invented. Note also what the pilot itself represents. Modern consulting increasingly sells tested proof rather than confident decks, especially to private equity owners who have been burned by big numbers before, and knowing how to design a clean experiment, controls, paired metrics so a win on cost cannot hide a loss on service, honest read periods, is becoming as core to this job as the spreadsheet. Priya's addition of turnover to the measures is the senior move worth studying: she is already building the slide that turns a cost fix into a budget victory for the team's strongest internal ally.",
    after: "Call ends 9:55. The rest of the morning belongs to the unglamorous work that keeps engagements from rotting: documentation, data hygiene, and promises turned into emails.",
  },
  "management-consultant-full-d5-b2": {
    before: "Nobody puts this on a recruiting poster, but it is as real as the boardroom: the Friday cleanup. The week generated nine deck versions, a model with a healed scar where $9.2M briefly became $9.7M, and several verbal promises that need to become written ones before they evaporate.",
    simulatedWork: "You work the list methodically, headphones on, coffee refilled. The labor model gets a documentation tab: every input sourced, the normalization explained, which version fed the SteerCo numbers, the footnote error and its fix logged like a flight incident. Future-you, four weeks from now, will need this the day Jake asks how a number was built, and \"I believe it was v6, possibly v7\" is not a sentence that survives contact with a sponsor.\n\nThe data request to Fresca IT goes out: daily overtime feeds for the ten pilot stores, mobile volumes, service times, numbered, specific, with a requested-by date, addressed to Gary, who answers one email a day, so this one is built to be the email. Calendar holds for the two regional interviews. The dead deck pages archived where they can be resurrected. Expenses submitted before finance starts naming names.\n\nAt 11:40, a different kind of email: the practice's staffing coordinator, subject line \"Availability check, June.\" Fresca runs five more weeks, but the machine is already asking what happens to you after it. You forward it to Priya with a question mark. Her reply, thirty seconds later: \"Standard. I already told them you're committed through Fresca and that they should want you after it. FYI: a good SteerCo travels fast internally. Enjoy that, quietly.\"",
    commentary: "Two systems are visible in this block, and both matter to your career more than any single deliverable. The first is hygiene: documented models, sourced numbers, specific data requests, promises converted to writing within a day. None of it is hard, all of it compounds, and senior people quietly keep a ledger of who they never have to chase, because in this industry brilliance gets noticed but reliability gets staffed. The second system is staffing itself, and it deserves demystifying: consulting firms run an internal marketplace where projects bid for people and people, within limits, position for projects. Between engagements sits \"the beach\", the unstaffed gap, restful for a few days and reputationally radioactive if it stretches, because an unbilled consultant is, in the firm's cold arithmetic, inventory. Your currency in that marketplace is exactly what Priya just spent on your behalf: the reputation compounding from weeks like this one. Veterans say it plainly, getting staffed well is a skill, and it is built on Fridays like this as much as Thursdays like yesterday.",
    after: "Inbox at zero, folder in order, a small glow from Priya's \"they should want you after it\" that you decide not to examine too closely. Lunch, and then a calendar invite Marcus sent from the flight home, titled only: \"coffee. real talk.\"",
  },
  "management-consultant-full-d5-b3": {
    before: "A coffee shop equidistant from both your apartments, no laptops, no agenda. Second-years do this for first-years a few months in: the unofficial download that HR does not schedule and could not script.",
    simulatedWork: "[Scene: a coffee shop equidistant from both your apartments. No laptops, no agenda. The unofficial download second-years give first-years a few months in. Two coffees in, Marcus gets to it.]\n\nMarcus (Associate): So. Three months, one SteerCo. Is it what you expected?\n\n[You give the honest answer: better than advertised on the learning and the people, harder than advertised on the 4:50 alarms, and you still can't tell if the speed of it is exhilarating or just loud. He nods like you've passed a small test.]\n\nMarcus (Associate): Both. Okay, here's the talk. The firm reviews everyone every six months. Committee of partners, your manager submits feedback, you get sorted into a bracket. Two \"belows\" in a row and you start hearing \"counseled out,\" which is the velvet term for being managed toward the exit with a runway and a reference. It's not rare. In any given class a real chunk of people don't make the two-year promotion, and it's usually not about intelligence. It's some mix of fit, sponsorship, and luck of the staffing draw.\n\nAnalyst (Consulting): That's bleak.\n\nMarcus (Associate): It's a system. Systems can be played. Rule one: your reviews are written by humans, so make sure humans with weight know your work. That's what Priya did for you this morning without being asked, by the way, and you should know that's gold. Rule two: take the feedback seriously but never personally. Rule three, (he turns his cup slowly) know why you're here. I'm eighteen months from the manager decision, and there's a PE portfolio-ops role circling me. Staying means I love THIS, the room, the rhythm, all of it. Leaving means this was a platform, which is what it is for most of us. Neither answer is failure. The only failure is not knowing which one is yours.\n\nAnalyst (Consulting): How do you find out?\n\nMarcus (Associate): Watch yourself on the good weeks. Yesterday was a good week. What was your first thought after the CEO wrote your number down?\n\n[You consider lying, and don't.]\n\nAnalyst (Consulting): More. I wanted more of that.\n\n[Marcus grins into his coffee.]\n\nMarcus (Associate): Yeah. That's data. Collect it for two years before you trust it.",
    commentary: "This conversation happens, in some form, in every consultant's first year, and it is worth more than any training module, so here is its content verified and unvarnished. The review machinery is real: semi-annual cycles, partner committees, performance brackets, and \"counseled out\" as the soft-exit mechanism of the up-or-out model, which the top firms run more rigorously than almost any industry. The non-meritocratic inputs Marcus named, staffing luck, sponsorship, the thin file after a slow quarter, are the parts veterans most wish someone had told them, because they convert a terrifying lottery into a playable system: do excellent work, ensure people with weight see it, metabolize feedback without spiraling. And the platform truth is the industry's most honest fact about itself: typical tenure is two to four years, the exits, private equity, strategy roles, startups, an outsized share of corporate leadership, are a feature the firms actively cultivate, and a minority stay because they genuinely love the room. Marcus's test, watch your own reaction to the good weeks, is as good a compass as exists, and \"I wanted more\" is exactly the kind of data point this entire platform was built to help you collect early, cheaply, and honestly.",
    after: "You walk home with more questions than you brought, which is the signature of a conversation that mattered. At 2:30: the firm's Friday training, executive communication, taught by a partner the first-years speak about in lowered voices.",
  },
  "management-consultant-full-d5-b4": {
    before: "Most major firms protect Friday afternoons for development, formal training layered on top of the daily apprenticeship. Today's session, live for all first-years in the office, is executive communication, taught by Elaine, a senior partner of the kind whose war stories have war stories. After yesterday's boardroom, the timing feels almost rude.",
    simulatedWork: "[Scene: the firm's protected Friday development session, executive communication, taught by Elaine, a senior partner whose war stories have war stories. No deck, which is its own statement. She teaches through stories and live drills.]\n\nElaine (Senior Partner): Executives do not have short attention spans. They have expensive ones. Your job is to be worth the next thirty seconds, thirty seconds at a time.\n\n[Then the drill: a volunteer summarizes a case in two minutes; she makes them do it in thirty seconds; then one sentence. The one-sentence versions, the room is unsettled to discover, are almost always better, and she lets the silence do the teaching.]\n\nElaine (Senior Partner): When you don't know, say so in five words and attach a date. \"I don't know, you'll have it Friday\" builds more boardroom trust than ten minutes of confident fog, because executives can smell fog from the hallway, and the first whiff costs you the deck.\n\n[You think of Dan at the dry run and realize you watched this law administered live. In the open drill, you retell yesterday's CEO moment as a case study, sixty seconds.]\n\nElaine (Senior Partner): Concrete, structured, brief. Now one sentence.\n\nAnalyst (Consulting): Their scheduling system went blind when the business went mobile, and fixing it is worth eleven million.\n\n[She points at you like a conductor cueing brass.]\n\nElaine (Senior Partner): THAT goes on a slide. Notice, everyone, what survived the compression: the mechanism and the money. That is always what survives.",
    commentary: "Two takeaways, one inside the content and one about its existence. The content itself, answer-first, ruthless compression, honest and dated \"I don't knows\", is the complete secret of executive communication, and consulting will drill it into you until it stops being technique and becomes personality; ask alumni what the job left permanently installed in them and this, not the financial modeling, is the most common answer, cited years later in jobs that have nothing to do with slides. The existence of the session is the second lesson: serious firms invest real, protected, recurring hours in formal development on top of the daily coaching, training weeks, Friday sessions, partner-taught masterclasses, and that investment is a substantial, never-itemized part of the compensation. You are being paid a strong salary to attend what amounts to a finishing school for professional skills, and the graduates carry the skills out the door, which the firms know, and fund anyway, because the alumni network is the marketing department.",
    after: "One meeting stands between you and the weekend: the Friday wrap with Priya, where the week officially closes and, this week, where the machinery Marcus described becomes visible.",
  },
  "management-consultant-full-d5-b5": {
    before: "Every Friday, a one-on-one with your engagement manager: structured feedback on the week, both directions. Week 3 was the largest of your short career, so today's runs long, and after the Marcus download, you finally understand what this meeting actually is: the place where your file gets written, one week at a time.",
    simulatedWork: "[Scene: the Friday one-on-one with your engagement manager. Structured feedback, both directions. After the Marcus download, you finally understand what this meeting is: the place where your file gets written, one week at a time. Priya does it the way the good ones do, specific, balanced, zero fog.]\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): What went well. You owned a workstream end to end: found the thread, took Monday's hole without sulking, rebuilt it stronger. Your store-visit instincts were real, Teresa told Rachel's people you actually listened, which travels further than you'd think. Thursday you were handed a hard moment in a CEO room and delivered a rehearsed answer that didn't sound rehearsed, which is the entire trick. Dan noticed. Dan does not always notice, and I'll be writing that down in the language the review committee reads.\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): What to work on, two things, and I mean them. One: push back earlier. You knew the operating-hours risk before Monday's session. I watched you present around it and wait for me to raise it. Trust the itch and say it first. Two: your so-whats are sharp by Thursday and mush on Tuesday. I want Tuesday-you writing Thursday-quality headlines. That's the next gear.\n\n[Then she flips it, as she does every week.]\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): What should I do differently?\n\n[You offer, carefully, that the 12:30 deadline on deck day was tighter than the 2 PM review required. She writes it down without a flicker of defense.]\n\nPriya (Engagement Manager): Fair. More buffer next SteerCo. Anything else? No? Then the official close. (she shuts her notebook) Good week. Genuinely. Nothing is due this weekend, and I'm instructing you, as your manager, not to open the model. Monday is a 6:05 flight and a pilot to design. Go be a person.",
    commentary: "This ritual is the engine of consulting's famous development speed, and now you can see its full anatomy: a named manager whose actual job includes making you better, specific behavioral feedback every single week, the same demanded in return, and, the part most first-years learn late, a direct pipeline from these Fridays into the semi-annual review file that partner committees read. Most industries deliver feedback twice a year through a form; this one delivers it weekly through a human who watched you work, and the compounding difference over two years is the whole reason the resume line is worth what it is worth. Notice the craft of the feedback itself, concrete behaviors, both directions, next gear named, and notice the final instruction. Sustainable consulting careers are co-authored: by managers who guard the off-switch and by juniors who actually flip it. The week is over. It is genuinely allowed to be over.",
    after: "Laptop closed at 5:10. One block remains, and it is not on any calendar.",
  },
  "management-consultant-full-d5-b6": {
    before: "Friday evening, your own couch, the city doing its Friday noise outside. Pizza ordered, the debt from Thursday honored. Your roommate asks how the week was, actually, and for once you try to answer it properly instead of with a shrug, because saying it out loud turns out to be how you find out what you think.",
    simulatedWork: "Friday evening, your own couch, the city doing its Friday noise outside. Pizza ordered, Thursday's debt honored. Your roommate asks how the week was, actually, and for once you try to answer it properly instead of with a shrug, because saying it out loud turns out to be how you find out what you think.\n\nYou lay the week out like exhibits. A Sunday-night suitcase and a 9:40 file marked \"nothing required tonight.\" A cliff found in a million rows before lunch on Monday, and a hole punched in it by 2. A general manager wrapping burritos at 8:40 because her software went blind two years ago, and a regional manager who only told the truth after you proved you'd watched. Three pages killed in nine seconds by a man who was right. A footnote error caught by a friend at 9:10 PM that would have been Thursday's whole meeting. Sixty rehearsed seconds in front of a CEO, and \"the most useful thing I've heard this month.\" A coffee where the most impressive person you know admitted he doesn't know if he's staying, and made not-knowing sound like wisdom.\n\nYour roommate listens all the way through and renders a verdict: \"So it's the most and least glamorous job ever invented, simultaneously.\"\n\n\"Yeah,\" you say, and realize it's the most precise summary anyone produced all week, including the deck. \"And I think I want to see week four.\"\n\nThat is the data point. You write nothing down. Some files keep themselves.",
    commentary: "If you compress this week, you get the honest trade of management consulting in one frame: genuine intellectual craft and absurd development speed, purchased with real hours, real travel, and a performance system that never fully sleeps; teams close enough to feel like a foxhole, inside a machine that will also, politely, manage you out if the file goes thin; moments of boardroom consequence that arrive years before any other career would grant them, funded by Sunday nights and 4:50 alarms. Some people read this week and feel exhausted; some feel a pull; many honest people feel both at once, and the proportion shifts week to week for years. Every reaction is data. The entire purpose of this simulation is to let you collect that data now, on a couch, for free, instead of eighteen months into a job that charges market rate for the lesson. Whatever you felt, write it down somewhere you will find it again. Then go look at another week, in another career, and compare the feelings, because that comparison, not any ranking or salary table, is what choosing well actually looks like.",
    after: "The week ends the way consulting weeks end: fully, and only briefly. Monday is a 6:05 flight, a pilot to design, ten stores to pick, and a number with your name quietly attached to it. The funnel is already drawn on a whiteboard in Columbus. And the strange truth you said out loud over pizza, the one this whole week was secretly measuring, is that some part of you is already looking forward to it.",
  },
}

